#version 450

// Phase 5, steps 5 and 7 -- vulkan-tutorial.com "Compute Shader":
// https://vulkan-tutorial.com/Compute_Shader
//
// Step 7 added the push constant and the scroll it drives. Everything else is
// step 5's and unchanged: at time == 0 this computes exactly what it did then,
// which is what keeps the startup self-test byte-exact without a special case.
//
// The first shader in this project that is not part of a drawing pipeline.
// There is no vertex to transform, no triangle, no fragment, no framebuffer.
// There is a grid of invocations and an image they write.

// THE WORKGROUP SIZE, and it is baked into the SPIR-V rather than passed in.
// A compute dispatch has two levels: vkCmdDispatch asks for a number of
// WORKGROUPS, and this line says how many INVOCATIONS are in each one. The
// total is the product, so 8*8 here and 100*75 groups there is 480,000
// invocations -- one per texel of an 800x600 image.
//
// Why the split exists: invocations within one workgroup run together on one
// compute unit, can share fast on-chip memory, and can synchronise with
// barrier(). Across workgroups none of that is true -- they may run in any
// order, on any unit, or not concurrently at all. 8x8 is the conventional
// choice for image work: 64 invocations is a multiple of both common hardware
// scheduling widths (32 on NVIDIA, 64 on AMD), and a square tile keeps the
// texels each group touches close together in memory, which matters a great
// deal once the image is tiled rather than linear.
//
// Nothing here is shared or synchronised yet. The path tracer is where it
// starts to matter: rays from neighbouring pixels hit the same BVH nodes, and
// a workgroup is the unit that could cache them.
layout(local_size_x = 8, local_size_y = 8, local_size_z = 1) in;

// NOT sampler2D. A storage image is bound as image2D, and the differences are
// not cosmetic:
//
//   sampler2D                    image2D
//   normalised UV coordinates    INTEGER texel coordinates
//   has a sampler                no sampler exists
//   filtering, address modes     neither -- no sampler to hold a policy
//   read with texture()          read/written with imageLoad/imageStore
//
// The `rgba8` is a FORMAT QUALIFIER and it is mandatory for a writeonly
// storage image: the shader has to be told how to pack the vec4 it stores
// into the image's actual bytes, because unlike a sampled read there is no
// sampler in the path to do the conversion. It must match the VkImage's
// format -- R8G8B8A8_UNORM here -- and a mismatch is undefined, not an error.
//
// `writeonly` is a promise that lets the compiler skip read paths. Drop it and
// this still works; claim it and then call imageLoad and the compile fails.
layout(binding = 0, rgba8) uniform writeonly image2D outImage;

// PUSH CONSTANTS. A small block of bytes that travels inside the COMMAND
// BUFFER itself -- not in a buffer, not in a descriptor, not allocated
// anywhere. vkCmdPushConstants records the value into the command stream, so
// the number is baked into the recorded commands. There is nothing to
// allocate, nothing to map, no descriptor to write, and nothing to
// synchronise: a value recorded into this frame's commands cannot be seen by
// the frame still executing.
//
// The budget is tiny by spec -- at least 128 bytes guaranteed, and the
// device's real figure is printed at startup. That is the whole trade: no
// setup cost at all, in exchange for a size measured in dozens of bytes. Four
// bytes of elapsed time is exactly the shape it is for.
//
// The alternative is the uniform buffer step 6 deleted: one buffer per frame
// in flight, a descriptor each, a persistent mapping, and a write that has to
// be ordered against the frame still reading the previous value.
layout(push_constant) uniform PushConstants {
    float time;
} push;

void main() {
    // WHERE AM I. gl_GlobalInvocationID is this invocation's position in the
    // whole dispatch grid: gl_WorkGroupID * gl_WorkGroupSize +
    // gl_LocalInvocationID, precomputed. It is a uvec3 because a dispatch is
    // always three-dimensional; z is 1 here and ignored.
    //
    // This is the entire input to a compute shader. There is no equivalent of
    // a vertex's attributes being fed in -- the invocation is handed its own
    // index and has to derive everything else from it. For a path tracer that
    // index IS the pixel, and the ray is computed from it.
    ivec2 coord = ivec2(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy);

    // The shader can ask the image how big it is, so the dimensions need no
    // uniform and cannot drift out of sync with the C++ side.
    ivec2 size = imageSize(outImage);

    // THE BOUNDS GUARD. Dead at this resolution and deliberately kept.
    //
    // A dispatch can only ask for whole workgroups, so a 801-wide image needs
    // 101 groups of 8 and gets 808 invocations across -- seven of which have
    // no texel. 800 and 600 are both multiples of 8, so right now this branch
    // never fires. It stays because the first thing that will change here is
    // the resolution, and a missing guard is the classic compute bug: the
    // stray invocations write outside the image, which for imageStore is
    // silently discarded rather than diagnosed, so the only symptom is
    // whatever the extra work cost.
    if (coord.x >= size.x || coord.y >= size.y) {
        return;
    }

    // INTEGER MATH DECIDES THE BYTES, on purpose.
    //
    // The obvious gradient is float(coord.x) / float(size.x - 1), and it would
    // look identical. The checkpoint for this step is a byte-for-byte
    // comparison against the same formula evaluated on the CPU, and a float
    // divide is specified to within 2.5 ULP in GLSL while the UNORM conversion
    // rounds with an implementation-chosen tie rule. The margin works out
    // about eight times larger than the error at this size, so it would
    // almost certainly pass -- "almost certainly" is not what a checkpoint is
    // for.
    //
    // Doing the division in integers removes the question. float(r) / 255.0 is
    // not exact in binary, but it is within a whisker of r/255, and the UNORM
    // conversion rounds to nearest, so it lands on exactly r. The C++ side
    // runs the identical integer expression.
    //
    // A path tracer's output is genuinely float and will want an HDR format
    // and a tone map. This is a verification choice for one step, not a
    // pattern to keep.
    // THE SCROLL. 120 texels a second, wrapping at the image width, so the
    // gradient cycles every 800/120 = 6.67 seconds and the wrap shows up as a
    // hard vertical edge where 255 meets 0 -- motion that is unmistakable
    // rather than a slow fade nobody can be sure about.
    //
    // int() truncates, and so does the C++ (int) cast that mirrors it. Both
    // sides compute the same float product from the same float push constant,
    // so the shift they derive is bit-identical and the checkpoint stays
    // byte-exact at an ARBITRARY animated time, not only at t = 0.
    //
    // At time == 0 the shift is 0 and sx is coord.x, which reduces this to
    // step 5's formula exactly.
    int shift = int(push.time * 120.0) % size.x;

    // Only x scrolls. y is deliberately left alone so the two axes stay
    // distinguishable: a bug that swapped them would turn a horizontal slide
    // into a vertical one, which is obvious by eye, where a diagonal slide
    // would hide it.
    int sx = (coord.x + shift) % size.x;

    int r = (sx * 255) / (size.x - 1);
    int g = (coord.y * 255) / (size.y - 1);

    // Blue held at a constant 64 so no texel can come out as step 4's
    // sentinel (0,0,255,255). Red and green both reach 0 at the top-left
    // corner, so without this the corner texel would be the sentinel exactly
    // and "the dispatch never ran" would be indistinguishable from "the
    // dispatch ran" at the one place it is easiest to look.
    imageStore(outImage, coord,
               vec4(float(r) / 255.0, float(g) / 255.0, 64.0 / 255.0, 1.0));
}
